        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A commercial parking lot or industrial yard demands something residential sealers simply cannot deliver. Higher traffic volume, fuel spills, larger square footage, and longer reapplication intervals require contractor-grade chemistry. After evaluating 12 products with actual paving contractors on real commercial jobs, here's what we recommend — and what to avoid.
        </p>

        <!-- SECTION 1: Residential vs Commercial -->
        <div id="residential-vs-commercial" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Why Commercial Asphalt Sealers Are Different
          </h2>

          <p>
            The asphalt sealer at your local hardware store is formulated for a residential driveway that sees two cars and no chemical exposure. A <strong>commercial asphalt sealer</strong> has to handle fundamentally different demands: heavy delivery trucks, forklift traffic, daily fuel spills, deicing salt application, and surfaces measured in acres rather than square feet.
          </p>

          <p>
            The key differences come down to <strong>solids content</strong>, <strong>polymer modification</strong>, and <strong>chemical resistance</strong>. Commercial-grade products typically contain 30-40% solids versus 15-25% in residential formulas. Higher solids content means a thicker, more protective film per coat — which translates directly to fewer reapplication cycles over the life of the pavement.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Residential vs. Commercial: By the Numbers</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">2-3 yrs</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Residential reapplication cycle</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">4-6 yrs</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Commercial reapplication cycle</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">15-25%</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Residential solids content</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#FF4500]">30-40%</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Commercial solids content</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 2: Types -->
        <div id="types-of-sealers" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Types of Commercial Asphalt Sealers
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Refined Coal Tar Emulsion</h3>
          <p>
            The traditional contractor specification for commercial parking lots and fuel-exposed surfaces. Refined coal tar provides <strong>maximum chemical resistance</strong> to petroleum products — gasoline, diesel, motor oil, antifreeze — that would rapidly degrade asphalt emulsion products. Longevity is exceptional at 4-6 years on standard commercial surfaces. The regulatory caveat is significant: coal tar is banned in Washington, Minnesota, and multiple municipalities due to PAH runoff concerns. Always verify local compliance before specifying.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Polymer-Modified Asphalt Emulsion</h3>
          <p>
            The rapidly growing alternative to coal tar, especially in regulated markets. Polymer-modified asphalt emulsions add synthetic rubber polymers to the base formula, dramatically improving flexibility, crack resistance, and longevity compared to straight asphalt emulsion. The best polymer-modified products approach coal tar durability while maintaining a clean environmental profile. This is the <strong>best commercial asphalt sealer</strong> for projects with environmental constraints.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Standard Asphalt Emulsion (Commercial Grade)</h3>
          <p>
            Higher-solids versions of the familiar residential asphalt emulsion product. Commercial-grade asphalt emulsion products deliver 3-4 year durability on standard parking surfaces with moderate traffic. They're the most environmentally benign option and the easiest to specify on government and green-certified projects. Performance in fuel-exposed applications is limited compared to coal tar or polymer-modified products.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Acrylic-Fortified Emulsion</h3>
          <p>
            Hybrid products that add acrylic polymers to asphalt emulsion for improved UV resistance and color retention. Best suited for <strong>commercial properties where appearance is as important as protection</strong> — retail centers, hotel properties, upscale office parks. Not the best choice for high-fuel-exposure surfaces.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <table class="w-full text-left">
