        <!-- Maintenance Schedule Section -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            Building a Commercial Pavement Maintenance Schedule
          </h2>

          <p>
            A proactive pavement maintenance schedule extends commercial asphalt life significantly compared to reactive repair-only approaches. Here is the standard contractor-recommended commercial maintenance timeline:
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Year 1–2: New Asphalt Curing Period</h3>
          <p>
            New commercial asphalt should cure for 12–24 months before the first seal coat. Fresh asphalt contains volatile oils that must evaporate and oxidize before sealing. Sealing too early traps these volatiles, preventing full hardening and potentially causing surface softness. During this period, fill any settlement cracks that appear and allow the surface to establish its final grade.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Year 2–3: First Seal Coat</h3>
          <p>
            Apply the first commercial seal coat after the initial curing period. This first coat is the most important application — it seals the oxidizing surface before significant binder degradation has occurred, dramatically slowing the aging process. Use a premium polymer-modified or coal tar product (where permitted) for this critical first application.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Year 6–7: Second Seal Coat</h3>
          <p>
            Reapply sealer when the first coat shows signs of wear — surface color fading, water no longer beading, or minor surface raveling in high-traffic areas. At this application, inspect for cracks that have developed and fill them before sealing. The fill-then-seal sequence is mandatory — sealing over unfilled cracks results in crack propagation through the sealer film within one season.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Year 10–12: Crack Seal and Reseal</h3>
          <p>
            By the 10-year mark on a well-maintained commercial lot, crack filling becomes a more significant scope item. Structural cracks (alligator cracking, edge cracking) that have developed indicate base issues that sealing alone cannot address — these areas require patching or mill-and-overlay before the seal coat. After patching and crack filling, apply a fresh two-coat seal application to restore full surface protection.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-blue-50 border-l-4 border-blue-500 rounded-r-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-sm font-black text-blue-800 mb-2">Annual Inspection Matters</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-blue-700">
              Even with a 4–5 year sealing schedule, commercial lots benefit from annual pavement inspections to catch developing cracks before they propagate into structural failures. Crack filling at the hairline stage costs a fraction of the cost of filling the same crack after one winter of freeze-thaw cycling has expanded it to a full structural breach.
            </p>
          </div>

          <p>
            The most important financial discipline in commercial pavement management is distinguishing between <strong>preventive maintenance</strong> (sealing and crack filling on a schedule) and <strong>deferred reactive repair</strong> (patching and overlay work triggered by visible failure). Properties that invest consistently in preventive maintenance rarely face the large capital expenditure of full lot replacement within a 25–30 year period. Properties that defer sealing to cut annual operating costs reliably face full replacement within 15 years at several times the cumulative cost of a prevention program. Document your sealing history with photographs, application dates, and product specifications — this record becomes a valuable asset when evaluating contractor bids, demonstrating due diligence to property ownership, and negotiating maintenance contracts with incoming paving contractors who need to fully understand the surface history and prior product specifications before confidently recommending the correct sealer product type, dilution ratio, and full application approach for the next scheduled maintenance cycle.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Related Guides -->
        <!-- Common Mistakes Section -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            Common Commercial Sealcoating Mistakes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Commercial lots differ from driveways mainly in scale, traffic, and the cost of getting it wrong. The errors are the same ones, with bigger consequences.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Over-Diluting to Stretch Coverage</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Manufacturer dilution rates exist because there is a minimum solids content below which the coat no longer protects. Adding water beyond that spec spreads further and wears out in a fraction of the time, which shows up as a lot that needs redoing in eighteen months instead of four years.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing Without Repairing First</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                On a commercial lot the cracks and potholes are where the water gets to the base, and sealer does nothing about either. Crack filling and pothole repair have to come first, and skipping them to hit a price point simply defers a much larger repaving bill.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Not Planning the Traffic Closure</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Sealer needs 24 to 48 hours before vehicles return, and a lot that reopens early gets tyre scuffs, tracking, and power-steering marks across the new coat. Sequence the work in sections and plan the closure with the tenant before the crew arrives, not on the day.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Applying Sand-Filled Sealer Where Traction Is Not Needed</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Sand-filled sealers add grip and wear resistance, which suits ramps, drive lanes, and slopes. On smooth pedestrian areas the same product shows brush marks and wears unevenly. Specify filled and unfilled by zone rather than coating the whole site identically.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Can I apply commercial asphalt sealer myself?</h3>
